  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between the compound (3S,4aR,6aR,6bS,8aS,12aR,14aR,14bR)-8a-(hydroxymethyl)-4,4,6a,6b,11,11,14b-heptamethyl-1,2,3,4a,5,6,7,8,9,10,12,12a,14,14a-tetradecahydropicen-3-ol and the CD276 antigen. This is supported by the fact that the compound is identified as a target of two different molecules, both of which are known to interact with the CD276 antigen. The first molecule, Methionine aminopeptidase 2, is a well-characterized enzyme that has been shown to be a target of the CD276 antigen, while the second molecule, [(1S,2R,3R,4R,5R,6S,7S,8R,9R,10R,13R,14R,16S,17S,18R)-5,14-diacetyloxy-11-ethyl-7,8-dihydroxy-6,16,18-trimethoxy-13-(methoxymethyl)-11-azahexacyclo[7.7.2.12,5.01,10.03,8.013,17]nonadecan-4-yl] benzoate, is also known to interact with the CD276 antigen. The evidence suggests that the compound may be a potential target for therapeutic intervention, as it is involved in the interaction with the CD276 antigen. The strength of the evidence is moderate, as the relationship is inferred through the indirect pathway of target engagement, and further experimental validation would be needed to confirm the direct interaction.",
